ing Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["Instruction, supervision, environment, and progressive exposure are relevant safeguards.","Use a clear stable floor or mat, optional hand support, controlled range, communication, and an exit without demographic classification."]},{"sectionKey":"dosage","sourceUrl":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29063454/","sourceTitle":"Acute Effects of Dynamic Stretching on Muscle Flexibility and Performance: An Analysis of the Current Literature","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["Dynamic-stretching dose varies across protocols.","Candidate cycle and range proposals remain review-only and stop before forced knee-to-floor pressure, hip or knee pain, loss of posture, or compensatory momentum."]},{"sectionKey":"instructions","sourceUrl":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24149201/","sourceTitle":"Effects of dynamic and static stretching within general and activity specific warm-up protocols","sourcePublisher":"Journal of Sports Science and Medicine","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["Implementation matters to the activity that follows.","Cue a seated setup, comfortable 90/90 positions, optional light hands behind, tall trunk, slow side-to-side switch, and no added rise without promising transfer."]},{"sectionKey":"safety_stop_rules","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/globalassets/about/position-statements/position_stand_youth_resistance_training---2009.pdf","sourceTitle":"Youth Resistance Training: Updated Position Statement Paper From the NSCA","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["Safe training requires appropriate instruction and environment.","Stop for pain, neurologic or circulation symptoms, dizziness, unsafe floor/support, inability to communicate, or participant request."]},{"sectionKey":"pr